  "result": "Neither. \"More agents always give better results\" is false, so five versus ten is the wrong question.\n\nYou've probably seen multi-agent demos that look impressive and assumed the number of agents is what made them work. Every extra agent adds its own model calls, and each one re-reads its own context. Agents also have to pass information to each other, and each handoff can drop details. That means higher cost and more places to fail, which matters on your small budget.\n\nSplitting pays off when the subtasks are independent, or when one context would get too big for a single agent. Your assistant does three things:\n\n- Answering from manuals\n- Checking the calendar\n- Booking\n\nThese are tools plus a few steps that always happen in the same order. A single agent with three well-described tools, or a fixed workflow around it, is the right starting point. Add a second agent only if you can name a specific problem the first one can't handle.\n\nDo you want a short lesson on when multi-agent designs are worth it?",
